David Tillotson wrote:

> Monsters are eligible for generation between from the highest eligible 
> level divided by 6 to the highest eligible level. The chances of 
> generation within those bounds are not affected by your experience 
> level. So once the highest monsters are eligible you have to gain 12 
> experience levels to change the monster generation by removing one level 
> of easy monsters. Well worth it, in my view.

Low level is only based on the dungeon level not by character level. The 
code is:

zlevel = level_difficulty();
/* determine the level of the weakest monster to make. */
minmlev = zlevel / 6;
/* determine the level of the strongest monster to make. */
maxmlev = (zlevel + u.ulevel) / 2;

So the lower bound is level_difficulty divided by 6 and upper bound is 
half of level_difficulty and character level added.

level_difficulty is defined as:

/* use instead of depth() wherever a degree of difficulty is made
  * dependent on the location in the dungeon (eg. monster creation).
  */
xchar
level_difficulty()
{
	if (In_endgame(&u.uz))
		return((xchar)(depth(&sanctum_level) + u.ulevel/2));
	else
		if (u.uhave.amulet)
			return(deepest_lev_reached(FALSE));
		else
			return((xchar) depth(&u.uz));
}

So on the planes it's sanctum level plus half of the character level.

Other places it's the lowest level reached (usually sanctum but not 
always) when you have the amulet otherwise it's the depth of the level 
where you are.

So leveling the character up has nothing to do on how low level monsters 
are generated unless the character is on planes.

The frequency of any one monster is not adjusted at all.

